 had opened. Her lab coat fell to her ankles. A gust of wind whipped the =
terrace, pushing her shoulder-length hair across the dark skin of her face.
"Yes, Doctor Blackwell," she replied.
[Elena didn't sound like herself. =
*Nervous*, Blackwell thought, *and she should be*.]
"The Mnemosyne Engine is connected. Those city workers over at Main Fusion =
didn't like the request. But, you've got the three hundred and twenty =
terawatts," she said.
Raising his cigarette to his lips one last time, the =
doctor inhaled until the end burned close to the filter. He held the smoke =
in his lungs for another long second, then exhaled and dropped the butt =
over the edge of the railing.
The spent cigarette vanished into the fog. =
[To Blackwell, the cloud cover was a necessary filter. It formed an opaque =
floor between him and the corroded foundations of the lower city.]
[The Dead Circuits. Crushed remains of Oshima city. Decades ago, =
Takeda-Holden hadn't bothered to demolish Oshima, they poured the concrete =
for New Veridia directly on top of it. He knew what lived down there in the=
 dark, buried beneath his feet: a tangle of failing systems, and the =
millions scurrying through the forgotten city. Noise beneath the sleeping =
monster.]
Blackwell turned and walked past Elena, stepping through the =
sliding glass doors into the corporate atrium. The multi-tiered annex was =
an expanse of sleek architecture. Sharp angles of polished concrete soared =
above and plunged below them.
The building's tinted black glass refracted =
the rising sun, its rays shifting down forty floors below them. Blackwell =
walked towards the executive lift. Elena followed, her posture rigid as the=
 steel elevator doors sealed them in. The high-speed descent was silent, =
dropping dozens of floors down, past the bottom of the main atrium and into=
 the Mnemosyne Lab.
